WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:07.590
In the sci-fi comedy Galaxy Quest, there’s
a scene where a human pretending to be an

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:13.960
alien is horrified by the food his species
is supposed to like.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.510
While the scene is played for laughs, it’s
interesting to think about the strange little

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.990
details of alien daily life, like what they
have for dinner, that most stories don’t

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:23.990
include.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:31.470
Enter The Birg Project, a worldbuilding series
that brings to life an expansive alien ecosystem,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:38.050
but also delves into the most minute cultural
details of its central intelligent species.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:43.350
Created by the brilliant artist Ripley, The
Birg Project doesn’t just explore the biology

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:50.600
of its titular aliens, but also imagines how
they put on clothes, how they play the horn,

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.390
even how they sneeze.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:58.060
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
explore the incredible world of the Birgs.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:04.220
And as usual, you can follow the artist on
social media and see more of her project using

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.820
the links in the description.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:12.800
Now, let’s explore this alien planet on
both a macro and micro scale…

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.710
We’ll start with the Birgs themselves.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:23.710
Also called ‘Birdbugs,’ these extraterrestrials
superficially resemble a cross between Earth

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:29.829
birds and Earth bugs — and have developed
a level of intelligence similar to our own.

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:36.140
The planet they evolved on is a habitable
world with extreme seasonal variations, meaning

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.180
the Birgs must hibernate for much of the year.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:45.220
Internally, Birg bones don’t all connect
in the way ours do, as the ancestor of most

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.880
land animals on their planet had an exoskeleton
like Earth insects.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:55.250
In the eons since, Birgs and their kin have
convergently developed vertebrae to support

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.720
their necks, but their ribs are still free-floating.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:05.540
Birg mouths are made of two nimble pinchers
that can move independently, as this biomechanical

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.140
visualization illustrates.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:14.160
This means that Birg jaws function more like
the mouthparts of Earth spiders than of vertebrates.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.330
Another visualization of Birg biomechanics
shows us how the creatures breathe: they take

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:25.780
in oxygen through apertures near their eyes,
which in turn lead to lungs located in their

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:30.420
neck, and they then exhale from exhaust apertures
on their sides.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.620
And as mentioned before, here’s how a Birg
sneezes…

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:40.769
Like humans, Birgs have a range of facial
expressions they use to communicate emotion.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:47.040
To our eyes, these expressions might be hard
to read, but to Birgs, these emotive displays

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.670
are just as intricate as our own — with
mandibles, eyes, and antennae position coming

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.530
into play.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:03:00.220
You may have already noticed that most Birgs
possess fine hairs over much of their bodies,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:05.500
which helps them keep warm during the long
winter months of their home world.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.980
Much of the Birg’s rounded shape actually
comes from this fluff, and without it they

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.080
appear like withered, skeletal lifeforms.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.750
Because of their fur, clothing isn’t the
same kind of cultural requirement it is in

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.730
most human societies.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:25.371
Yet many Birgs still wear garments to further
shield themselves from the elements, show

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.370
off their wealth, or denote their nationality.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.370
For Birg societies are just as complex as
our own...

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:40.450
And like humans, Birg civilization has been
shaped in no small part by the numerous other

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.170
species on their planet.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:48.850
Trunkhorses are among the most invaluable
species to Birg society, serving as both steeds

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.450
and guardians of Birg children.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:57.170
This mare keeps a watchful eye on her foals
as she grazes — and while one of her foals

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.690
looks different than the others, she guards
it just as fiercely.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:07.420
Another steadfast companion of the Birgs are
the Jaubja, dog-like creatures that have been

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.200
at the Birg’s side since the dawn of their
civilization.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:17.329
Like Earth dogs, many breeds of Jaubja exist
— with most acting as pest control and highly

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:20.139
territorial guardians of farmland.

00:04:20.139 --> 00:04:26.860
Like cats, Jaubja are largely self-domesticated
— meaning they chose to integrate with Birg

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.009
society as a survival strategy.

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:32.400
And Birgs keep other types of pets as well.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.830
Here, a child proudly displays their prized
Scalebugs.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:42.169
These colorful creatures spend their larval
stage in the bark of certain trees, where

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:46.479
they are collected by local kids and raised
to their adult stage.

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:52.979
These vibrant pets are considered fashionable,
and function almost like living jewelry.

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:58.020
Birgs also cultivate insects called ‘pottery
bees’ in large farms.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.300
The bees themselves are rarely consumed, but
a fungus that the bees grow for food is considered

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.020
a regional delicacy.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:11.820
Populations are carefully maintained by local
keepers, protecting the large mud nests with

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.570
fences and moats.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:19.470
It is custom for pottery bee-keepers to leave
a small token as “payment” for the harvested

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:22.390
product.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:29.460
On farms, the larger ‘Bumblebird’ classification
of insects plays key roles as pollinators.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:35.150
Many plants have co-evolved pits full of sugary
reservoirs to lure in Bumblebirds, who then

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:40.130
spread their pollen to other plants — a
convergent relationship to that of flowers

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:43.080
and Earth bumblebees.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:48.370
Most insects on the Birg planet are covered
in scales, which have developed into protective

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:51.370
exoskeletons in some clades.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:57.550
Many flying clades of insects lay their larvae
within specific crops, and sometimes play

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.360
a vital role in the life-cycle of the plants.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.690
These strange parasites are a hugely important
resource to the Birgs both as pollinators

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:13.520
and as food—and are also consumed for their
mind-altering properties.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:18.650
For Birgs who live on the coasts, most of
their food comes not from the land, but from

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:19.750
the sea.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:25.720
Coastal Birgs partake in a wide variety of
seafood fresh from the planet’s bountiful

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:26.720
oceans.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.720
Fish within the waters of the Birg homeworld
take on a variety of curious and startling

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.280
forms, with most possessing the multi-part
mouths common throughout most lineages on

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.570
the planet.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:44.889
Some of the largest creatures in the ocean
are filter-feeders with specialized mouths

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:49.599
that funnel zooplankton into baleen like structures
on their jowls.

00:06:49.599 --> 00:06:54.919
While these creatures might not look it, they
share a common ancestor with the dog-like

00:06:54.919 --> 00:06:57.710
Jaubja that the Birgs keep as pets.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:04.180
Elsewhere on the coast, there’s also a species
of crab-like lifeforms — which is good,

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:09.110
because all speculative settings should have
some form of crab.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:15.660
Further inland, the Flagdeer family sports
some of the tallest creatures found on Birgworld.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.840
These towering lifeforms are related to the
Trunkhorses, although their trunks have become

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:26.430
lengthy, flagpole-like appendages used to
reach leaves on the tops of trees — much

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:29.470
like the long necks of Earth giraffes.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:35.720
On the opposite end of the spectrum, snuffling
around the bottoms of trees are pig-like,

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:39.419
armored herbivores that feed on bark and roots.

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:45.110
Their sharp mandibles can inflict serious
harm on would-be predators, so most creatures

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:48.030
keep their distance.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:54.040
The tree-like plants of the Birg planet are
quite different from trees here on Earth.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:59.150
Sun-spires are towering plants with leaves
that grow in tightly clustered chambers like

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.330
giant compound eyes.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:07.370
These lenses act like a magnifying glass,
helping to optimize photosynthesis, and the

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:13.480
Birgs once used them to make decorative windows,
sun catchers, and even primitive spectacles

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:14.949
and telescopes.

00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:20.389
Today, Sun-spire lenses are more commonly
farmed to make distinctive jewelry.

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:26.630
A ubiquitous group of trees are the Feathervanes,
plants that are named for their feather-like

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.449
assemblage of leaves.

00:08:28.449 --> 00:08:33.959
Though equivalent to Earth trees in certain
ways, Feathervanes diverge in their production

00:08:33.959 --> 00:08:39.050
of ‘walking seeds’ — a mobile stage
of their reproductive cycle that can amble

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:44.491
along in search of ideal land in which to
begin the more familiar ‘plant’ stage

00:08:44.491 --> 00:08:46.470
of their lifecycle.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:52.330
Different walking seeds have different specializations,
and most briefly fill an insect-like niche

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:53.330
within their ecosystem.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:09:00.470
In silica rich soils, landscapes of coiling
vines dominate the scenery.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:05.680
These mounds take thousands of years to reach
their current size, with successive layers

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.850
of vines growing on top of the old ones in
a reef-like fashion.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:16.350
And like marine reefs, they support a diverse
assemblage of life, including local Birgs

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.320
that hunt for small game.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.260
Yet Birgs aren’t always the ones doing the
hunting.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:27.910
Many Birgs are still very much in the middle
of a larger food web, and there are various

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:33.430
apex predator species that can turn a Birg
into their lunch — especially during the

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:36.720
planet’s long winters when Birgs need to
hibernate.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.329
And there is no deadlier predator of the hibernation
period than the Omb.

00:09:42.329 --> 00:09:47.540
The source of the Birg’s darkest legends,
these beasts have sensitive mechanoreceptors

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:52.760
in their legs that allow them to locate burrows
hidden underground.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:58.160
The Omb’s habit of exhuming hibernating
animals has inspired countless horror stories

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.980
of Birgs’ homes being torn into in the dead
of the night…

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:09.070
For even at mid-latitudes, Birgworld winters
are easily as cold, dark, and vicious as those

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:12.060
seen in the far polar regions of Earth.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:17.829
Nothing roams these snows save the lumbering
beasts that prey on the bones of the dead

00:10:17.829 --> 00:10:23.130
as readily as they wrest sleeping creatures
from their hidden burrows.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:34.370
Aesthetically and thematically, the Birg project
takes inspiration from the 1984 Hayao Miyazakifilm

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:37.140
Nausicaä of the Valley of the Wind.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:42.940
Based on a manga of the same name, the film
tells the story of a post-apocalyptic Earth

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:49.519
with a toxic ecosystem, where the last humans
struggle to coexist with giant insects.

00:10:49.519 --> 00:10:55.439
The visual parallels between the surreal,
insect-dominated ecosystems of Nausicaä and

00:10:55.439 --> 00:10:58.149
the Birg project are evocative.

00:10:58.149 --> 00:11:03.860
During the early stages of production, director
Miyazaki created several water color paintings

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:09.399
to help decide the look of his futuristic
biosphere, and these early storyboards in

00:11:09.399 --> 00:11:13.889
particular echo through the style of the Birg’s
planet.

00:11:13.889 --> 00:11:20.270
In different ways, both stories explore themes
of conflict between society and nature.

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:25.540
Again and again in Nausicaä, humans are reminded
that the Earth is something beyond their control,

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:28.700
and trying to resist this only ends in self-destruction.

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:35.540
Likewise, for all their intelligence, the
Birgs don’t have dominion over their unforgiving

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:36.540
planet.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:41.780
They are just a single thread in a larger
ecological tapestry, with all aspects of their

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:46.839
culture shaped by their environment…

00:11:46.839 --> 00:11:52.399
Yet different Birg civilizations have different
relationships with nature, as Birg cultures

00:11:52.399 --> 00:11:55.540
are as rich and varied as our own.

00:11:55.540 --> 00:12:01.040
One of the most influential groups are the
Twowi — Birgs who control a vast empire

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:02.960
of interconnected cities.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:09.139
Culture across the Twowi kingdom is endlessly
complex, with an emphasis on folklore and

00:12:09.139 --> 00:12:12.190
ceremonial formalities.

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:17.810
When a Twowi dies, family members carry the
body to a special room, where it naturally

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:22.209
mummifies, and they keep in there for a year-long
grieving period.

00:12:22.209 --> 00:12:27.660
The body is then cremated and spread to the
wind, with any remaining ashes mixed into

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:33.560
the clay of a little sculpture made to resemble
the departed loved-one.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:38.740
Most Twowi buildings are wattle and daub,
made from the grey-bluish wood of feathervane

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:44.470
trees and bundles of fibrous grass stuffed
into walls for insulation.

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:50.199
For food, one of the key staple crops in the
Twowi empire are a tuber-like “fungus”

00:12:50.199 --> 00:12:53.550
that act as roots to the feathervane trees.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:58.680
With their agriculture and city building,
the Twowi are quite different from most nomadic

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.519
Birg cultures.

00:13:00.519 --> 00:13:05.850
Yet the Twowi still engage in many traditional
Birg practices, like the grooming of each

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:10.210
other’s fuzz to remove dirt and unwanted
parasites.

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:15.639
This social grooming behavior is not unlike
that of certain Earth primates, and is a way

00:13:15.639 --> 00:13:19.980
for the Birgs to deepen their bonds.

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:25.420
Some of the most esteemed community members
are Moonwatchers, shamans who forgo winter

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:31.190
hibernation in order to guard their sleeping
communities from the physical and spiritual

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.899
horrors of the long cold night.

00:13:33.899 --> 00:13:41.070
For even in the Twowi empire, Birgs are never
fully separate from nature.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:45.649
Of course, Twowi aren’t the only Birgs with
distinct cultures.

00:13:45.649 --> 00:13:52.010
Some Birg societies practice scarification,
using scaring to creature cosmetic patterns

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:53.310
in their fuzz growth.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:59.660
And most Birg societies have their own form
of music, like these ceremonial horns designed

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:02.380
to resemble the mouths of Trunkhorses.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:08.009
Due to Birgs’ unique biology, the ‘mouthpieces’
cover their exhaust spiracles.

00:14:08.009 --> 00:14:13.329
Unique instruments present in the Twowi empire
are standing devices functionally similar

00:14:13.329 --> 00:14:18.920
to the accordion, with bellows pumped by the
medial arms while the anterior hands press

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:21.570
the keys.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:29.110
Nearly all Birg societies engage in, uh, chemical
recreation — which can range from Grubfruits,

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:34.470
which have perception altering effects on
Birgs — to the shaved bark of certain plants,

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:36.899
which relaxes their body and mind.

00:14:36.899 --> 00:14:42.350
In Birg culture, these stimulants play an
important role in social bonding, and are

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:44.930
expected at most gatherings.

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:50.839
One of the weirder forms of recreation the
Birgs have involve slumber-bugs, scorpion-like

00:14:50.839 --> 00:14:56.089
creatures that produce venom which medics
have historically used as painkillers.

00:14:56.089 --> 00:15:02.410
However, some Birgs use them… less responsibly,
which over centuries has led to various breeds

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:05.860
with creative names and variations in potency.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:13.820
While not outlawed, slumber-bug use is widely
seen as a bad idea.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:19.860
Like many ancient and modern human cultures,
Birgs have created a wide array of mobility

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:20.860
aids.

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:26.240
Canes and crutches come in a few different
forms, with their shape depending on which

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.450
Birg limb needs the most support.

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:36.680
In the windy equatorial region, strange statues
suggest Birg civilization has a long history.

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:42.330
The Singing Giants, mysterious statues that
make an eerie whistling noise when the wind

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:46.500
blows through the holes in their heads, lie
scattered on the plains.

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:51.620
Their original purpose is unknown, but the
local nomads believe their humming are the

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:54.980
voices of passing ghosts.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:16:00.550
Much of Birg religion is tied to seasonal
changes and environmental forces.

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:06.170
Some communities participate in ritualistic
brush burning: removing dead grasses so new

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:08.400
ones can spring up.

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:14.120
At seasonal festivals, Birgs who wish to show
off don flashy costumes and circle around

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:16.270
each other in a mock battle.

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:21.360
Referees judge participants in these dancing
rituals on their synchronicity and how close

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:25.139
they stay to their partner without touching
them.

00:16:25.139 --> 00:16:31.709
And like all known human cultures, Birg societies
have legends of monsters, one of which this

00:16:31.709 --> 00:16:34.149
folk costume is meant to depict.

00:16:34.149 --> 00:16:38.730
As on Earth, most of these mythical beings
do not actually exist…

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:40.690
…But perhaps some do.

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:46.420
A widespread legend amoung Birgs tells of
an eerie creature, something like a great

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:50.690
worm with many legs that stalks the thickets
at night.

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:55.760
Some say they come from the underworld, or
perhaps they are mischievous spirits of the

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:56.760
wild.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:02.930
But in truth, these secretive, nocturnal lifeforms
are called Millipeebs — a highly intelligent

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:08.770
species that quietly coexist with the Birgs
in the remote corners of the planet.

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:12.329
Their exact intelligence is difficult to measure.

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:17.089
Perhaps they are as smart as dolphins — or
perhaps they’re just as intelligent as the

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:18.730
Birgs themselves.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:25.740
In any case, these wise, elusive beings have
stayed hidden from the Birgs for untold generations

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:29.760
by retreating into huge dirt mounds during
the day.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:35.460
Perhaps one day, as empires like the Twowi
continue to expand, a fateful meeting will

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:40.549
finally occur...

00:17:40.549 --> 00:17:45.880
Some of the most interesting pieces from the
Birg project depict the experiences of the

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:49.470
Twowi and other groups on the edge of the
frontier.

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:54.350
They show an almost mythic landscape of discovery
and danger.

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:59.980
Aesthetically, these works take inspiration
from a very unexpected source for a work of

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:05.040
speculative biology: the cinematic language
of westerns.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:10.800
One of the largest genres of film for over
half a century, classical westerns told stories

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:15.360
of exaggerated heroism and a mythical golden
age of freedom.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:21.280
In the modern era, however, so called neo-westerns
have sought to re-evaluate the folk stories

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:26.789
of the old west, examining the era under a
more self-aware and critical lens.

00:18:26.789 --> 00:18:32.710
While thematically the Birg project strongly
echoes the latter, aesthetically many pieces

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:38.390
pay clever homage to old-school pulp westerns
— taking the iconography of gunslingers,

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:44.870
horses, and outlaws and translating them into
a new dialect of alien imagery.

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:50.790
For example, one image translates the classic
trope of a cattle herder into something…

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:52.170
decidedly unique.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:57.050
Here, we see a shepherd on a Trunkhorse watching
over their livestock.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:02.730
These animals themselves aren’t valuable,
but instead serve as hosts to a sort of tick

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:07.020
that is sold as a delicacy in Twowi cities.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:13.179
On a dusty street corner in a city, an old
herder idles in the afternoon sun.

00:19:13.179 --> 00:19:19.480
Far from a heroic folk-tale, life in these
settlements is often tough and short.

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:24.970
In the Twowi borderlands, a proud captain
bears the flag of her modest gang.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:30.919
Rear end adornments are ubiquitous in Twowi
society, but flags are more formal, used to

00:19:30.919 --> 00:19:36.559
identify important members in war parties
and to distinguish friend from foe.

00:19:36.559 --> 00:19:41.030
Twowi who venture beyond city limits must
come prepared.

00:19:41.030 --> 00:19:45.630
Headgear for shielding the eyes and saddles
adorned with packets for carrying personal

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.530
items are key.

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:52.799
It is also common to wear underclothes to
stay protected from the wind and dust.

00:19:52.799 --> 00:19:57.330
This particular Birg doing the modeling is
named ‘Swooce’ — and she is something

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:02.610
of a protagonist in this strange, neo-western
mythos.

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:08.230
In one of the most expressive pieces, Swooce
races through the canyonlands, having drawn

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:11.340
the ire of a large aerial predator.

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:15.230
While she still has her firearm, it’s not
doing her much good.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:21.409
Like most neo-western protagonists, Swooce
experiences more misfortune on the frontier

00:20:21.409 --> 00:20:23.640
than dazzling heroics.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:29.800
More broadly, this section of the Birg project
echoes the neo-western motifs of ordinary

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:37.250
people trying to live ordinary lives yet struggling
to survive…

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:43.240
It seems fitting that a project with an emphasis
on minute details would take inspiration from

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:46.740
a genre built on stories of ordinary lives.

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:51.990
Yet the Birg project is too unique to be pinned
down to any single genre.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:57.170
The artist states that they feel the project
has expanded beyond its western roots, and

00:20:57.170 --> 00:21:02.679
has become now a broader exploration of how
people interact with and are shaped culturally

00:21:02.679 --> 00:21:08.860
by their environments — even if, in this
case, the ‘people’ are six-limbed aliens.

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:13.520
If you find this project as fascinating as
I do, you can support the artist on social

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:17.350
media and see more of the Birgs using the
links in the description.

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:21.650
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:26.490
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:31.159
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:21:31.159 --> 00:21:39.979
See you in the next video.

